\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Comments by Pierre Karl P\'e9ladeau, President and Chief Executive Officer of Quebecor}{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard
\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2
\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Quebecor continues to demonstrate strong financial performance, quarter after quarter. In the third quarter of 2025, it posted an 18.7% increase in free cash flows from operating activities; a 5.7% increase in adjusted EBITDA, which was up across all business segments; a 1.1% increase in revenues and a 25.7% increase in adjusted net income. On the strength of these excellent results, driven by rigorous, disciplined management, we were able to reduce our consolidated net debt by more than $300 million in the third quarter of 2025 and nearly $700 million over the past 12 months. We lowered our consolidated net debt leverage ratio to 3.03x as of September 30, 2025, the lowest among Canada\u8217 s main telecommunications providers.}
{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Pierre Karl P\'e9ladeau    ,\~President and Chief Executive Officer of Quebecor}{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0
\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The Telecommunications segment maintained its momentum in the third quarter of 2025 with increases of 2.8% in adjusted EBITDA, 6.4% in mobile telephony service revenues, 1.1% in total revenues, and 0.8% in adjusted cash flows from operations, while increasing investments by $13\~million during the quarter. This success was based, first and foremost, on our ability to attract and retain an ever\u8209 growing customer base with a range of innovative products at highly competitive prices. Our mobility brands continued to gain significant market share across Canada, adding 113,800\~lines (2.7%) during the quarter and 323,100\~lines (8.1%) over the past 12\~months, the strongest growth rates in the Canadian industry.    We continued expanding our Helix Internet and television services during the third quarter of 2025. These services are now available to more than 180,000\~additional households across many Qu\'e9bec communities, including Drummondville, Magog, Rimouski, Saint\u8209 Hyacinthe, Trois\u8209 Rivi\'e8res, Salaberry\u8209 de\u8209 Valleyfield and Huntingdon, as well as in many cities in Saguenay\u8211 Lac-Saint-Jean. In line with our commitment to improving access to mobile communications in outlying regions, Videotron expanded its wireless coverage in the Haute\u8209 Mauricie region, in partnership with Ecotel\~Inc. and with support from the Qu\'e9bec government. More than 10,000\~additional residents can now subscribe to Videotron\u8217 s mobile services. Meanwhile, Freedom Mobile expanded its service area to include the Chatham\u8209 Kent region in Ontario. Residents can now access our competitive plans, including the innovative Roam Beyond plan, delivered via a fast and reliable network. As a result of the series of service area expansions in recent quarters, combined with roaming agreements under the Canadian Radio\u8209 television and Telecommunications Commission\u8217 s (CRTC) Mobile Virtual Network Operator (MVNO) framework, Videotron, Fizz and Freedom now reach over 83% of Canada\u8217 s population.}
{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 We are particularly proud of the results of the latest L\'e9ger survey, which show that Videotron remains the undisputed leader in customer service among telecom providers in Qu\'e9bec. Videotron was also rated the most reliable and trustworthy telecom, attesting to the superb customer experience we deliver, day after day.}
{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In October\~2025, Videotron announced the pricing of its $800.0\~million private placement of 3.95% Senior Notes maturing in 2032. The success of this offering confirmed Videotron\u8217 s status as one of the leaders in Canadian telecommunications provider. Strong investor demand on attractive terms for Videotron, including the lowest seven\u8209 year credit spread in the Canadian telecom sector, testifies to our solid financial foundations, disciplined management and growth prospects.}
{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 TVA\~Group\~Inc. (\u8220 TVA\~Group\u8221 ) generated revenues of $106.2\~million in the third quarter of 2025, down $6.2\~million (\u8209 5.5%), and year\u8209 to\u8209 date revenues of $355.3\~million, down $30.3\~million (\u8209 7.8%). Although TVA\~Group posted adjusted EBITDA of $18.5\~million in the third quarter of 2025, thanks to numerous restructuring measures over the past two years and certain non\u8209 recurring favourable retroactive adjustments in 2025, this was not enough to achieve profitability for the first nine months of the year. These measures are not sustainable in the medium term and are far from sufficient to secure the long\u8209 term viability of our business, given in particular the ongoing, accelerating decline in advertising revenues, compounded by the absence of foreign blockbusters at MELS\u8217  studios. It is therefore imperative that governments also act and take the necessary steps to support domestic media companies in the long term.    Unfortunately, the federal\~government has completely ignored our industry and turned a blind eye to the crisis that is hitting television broadcasting so hard. There is no tax credit for television journalism, no tax incentives for advertising in Qu\'e9bec and Canadian media, and no information about when the digital services tax already paid by private broadcasters will be refunded. Furthermore, CBC/Radio\u8209 Canada\u8217 s annual funding has been increased by $150 million without any requirement to eliminate advertising on its platforms or to curb its unfair commercial competition with Canada\u8217 s private television broadcasters. Regrettably, this new government has missed an opportunity to support an industry facing ever-growing challenges and job losses at an alarming rate. Regarding the Qu\'e9bec government, we reiterate that it must quickly introduce concrete measures to implement the recommendations in the report of its task force on the future of Qu\'e9bec\u8217 s audiovisual industry, filed in October\~2025.}
{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 TVA\~Group\u8217 s broadcasting activities continued to dominate television viewership in Qu\'e9bec. Its channels registered a combined market share of 41.2% in the third quarter of 2025, a 2.2\u8209 point increase from 2024 and more than its two main rivals combined. TVA Network maintained its lead among French\u8209 language over\u8209 the\u8209 air channels on the strength of programs such as Chanteurs masqu\'e9s, which drew an average audience of more than 1.5\~million for a 52.9% market share. Among the specialty channels, LCN remained Quebecers\u8217  trusted news source with an 8.0% market share, a 0.9\u8209 point increase over the same period of 2024, and TVA Sports recorded a significant 1.4\u8209 point gain in the third quarter of 2025 compared with the same period in 2024.    We proudly upheld our founder Pierre P\'e9ladeau\u8217 s commitment to actively contribute to the development and well\u8209 being of the community. Quebecor donated $2\~million to the Fondation Sablon, an organization that provides disadvantaged children with opportunities to grow through sports and outdoor activities. The donation will fund the complete refurbishment of the Centre aquatique Qu\'e9becor, improving access to quality facilities for young people and their families. We also continued to support university entrepreneurship, notably through the Pierre P\'e9ladeau Bursaries. In their 27th year, the Bursaries awarded more than $200,000, shared among five recipients, to encourage innovation, creativity and risk\u8209 taking by Qu\'e9bec student entrepreneurs.}
